```c
if (entry->gate_mask & BIT(tc)) {
    gate_len[tc] += entry->interval;
} else {
    if (min_gate_len[tc] > gate_len[tc])
        min_gate_len[tc] = gate_len[tc];
    gate_len[tc] = 0;
}
```
Reason:
The logic incorrectly considers a "gate close" event whenever a traffic class (TC) is detected as having a gate closed (i.e., a 0 in the gate mask). This level-triggered approach can lead to `min_gate_len[tc]` being set to 0 when a gate is closed without having been open prior, preventing it from tracking valid open intervals correctly.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (min_gate_len[tc] > gate_len[tc] && gate_len[tc])
    min_gate_len[tc] = gate_len[tc];
```
Reason:
This change makes the update to `min_gate_len[tc]` conditional on `gate_len[tc]` being non-zero, effectively making the logic edge-triggered. This prevents `min_gate_len[tc]` from being erroneously set to 0 when a gate closes without having been open, allowing it to correctly track the minimum length of continuously open gates. Additionally, the new logic ensures that permanently closed gates are handled appropriately by setting their `min_gate_len[tc]` to 0 after the loop if they were never opened.

